      "text": "To find \\(\\sin F\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\) where \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the type of triangle**: Since \\(\\sin E = 1\\), angle \\(E\\) must be \\(90^\\circ\\) because \\(\\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\). Therefore, triangle \\(DEF\\) is a right triangle with \\(\\angle E = 90^\\circ\\), \\(\\angle D\\) and \\(\\angle F\\) being the other two angles.\n\n2. **Use the Pythagorean identity**: For any right triangle, the sum of the squares of the sine and cosine of the angles is 1. This can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   \\sin^2 \\theta + \\cos^2 \\theta = 1\n   \\]\n   We know \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), so we can find \\(\\cos D\\) using the Pythagorean identity:\n   \\[\n   \\cos^2 D = 1 - \\sin^2 D = 1 - \\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 = 1 - \\frac{25}{169} = \\frac{144}{169}\n   \\]\n   Therefore,\n   \\[\n   \\cos D = \\pm \\frac{12}{13}\n   \\]\n   Since \\(D\\) is an angle in a right triangle, \\(\\cos D\\) must be positive. Thus,\n   \\[\n   \\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine \\(\\sin F\\)**: Since \\(F\\) is the right angle, \\(\\sin F = \\sin 90^\\circ = 1\\).\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(\\sin F\\) is \\(\\boxed{1}\\).",
